WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.130
“Ninety-eight per cent accurate!”

2
00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.010
That’s a company’s email to our

3
00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.490
school, about its AI for spotting

4
00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:09.070
pupils likely to drop out.

5
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.760
Ninety-eight per cent of what?

6
00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.950
Today: train a model, test it

7
00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:18.250
honestly, and question its score.

8
00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:22.190
Give a model lots of freedom and few

9
00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:25.050
examples, and it can bend to fit every

10
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.490
one, mistakes and noise included.

11
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.100
It scores perfectly on

12
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.060
those training examples.

13
00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:34.190
But on new ones, it can be badly wrong.

14
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.860
So before training, we put some examples

15
00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.680
aside: a test set the model never sees.

16
00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.390
The training score shows how well

17
00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:45.170
it fitted its own examples.

18
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.780
The test score shows how it’s

19
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.400
likely to do on new pupils.

20
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.540
This is overfitting: learning the

21
00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:56.260
training examples so closely that the

22
00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.060
model does worse on new ones.

23
00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:01.030
The warning sign: a high training

24
00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:03.330
score, and a much lower test score.

25
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:05.740
You’ve seen this in class.

26
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.170
One pupil memorises last year’s exam

27
00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:11.650
paper and scores full marks on it.

28
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.740
Another learns the methods.

29
00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:17.200
Give them both new questions, and you

30
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.020
soon see who really learned.

31
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:20.760
Quick check!

32
00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:23.810
A model scores a hundred per cent

33
00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:26.490
on its training examples, and

34
00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.950
seventy on its test set.

35
00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.420
Which score should you trust?

36
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.260
Seventy, from the test set.

37
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.900
The hundred only shows how well it

38
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.140
fitted its own examples, and the

39
00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:40.860
gap warns of overfitting.

40
00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.390
Even seventy holds only if the test

41
00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.170
examples look like your pupils.

42
00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.640
And every score hides two

43
00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:50.480
kinds of mistakes.

44
00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:52.550
Take the dropout tool.

45
00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.630
A false positive flags a pupil who

46
00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:57.070
was never going to leave.

47
00:01:57.790 --> 00:01:59.890
A false negative misses a

48
00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:01.310
pupil who does leave.

49
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:02.960
Which costs more?

50
00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:07.130
Usually the miss: that pupil got no help.

51
00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.110
But false flags cost too, if

52
00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:11.190
pupils get labelled.

53
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.960
Now, that ninety-eight per cent.

54
00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.870
If two pupils in a hundred drop out,

55
00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:20.570
a tool that always says “nobody

56
00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:22.990
leaves” is also ninety-eight per

57
00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:24.710
cent accurate, and useless.

58
00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.370
So ask three questions.

59
00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:29.200
Tested on whom?

60
00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:30.830
On which data?

61
00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:32.330
With which mistakes?

62
00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.940
In the lab, circles are training

63
00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.460
examples and squares are new ones.

64
00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:41.630
Give the model more freedom, and watch

65
00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:45.010
its curve chase every circle while the

66
00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:46.870
error on squares climbs.

67
00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.560
Then switch to many examples:

68
00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:51.260
the curve calms down.

69
00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:53.220
Your turn.

70
00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.940
The tool said a pupil was fine,

71
00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:57.920
but she left school in March.

72
00:02:58.650 --> 00:02:59.770
Which mistake was that?

73
00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.530
A false negative: the tool said

74
00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.750
no, but the answer was yes.

75
00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:08.620
Remember: a perfect training

76
00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:10.380
score can hide overfitting.

77
00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:12.310
Trust scores on examples

78
00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:13.650
the model never saw.

79
00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.360
And ask: tested on whom, on which

80
00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.400
data, with which mistakes?

81
00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.910
Try it in class tomorrow: play

82
00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:24.710
“Memoriser and learner”.

83
00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:27.730
One pupil memorises the answers to

84
00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.790
ten area questions; another learns

85
00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:31.970
how to work out area.

86
00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.780
Then ask them both new questions.

87
00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:37.710
The class sees overfitting for itself.
